    "turn_text": "Absolutely. And it comes back to me to the comms philosophy of positioning. This was a really a famous and useful concept that I have learned over the last five years which is every individual voter has some guitar chords in their head and the way to get a message imparted to them is not to play a song and hope that they just hear it. It's to pluck the guitar chords that exist in their head and so when NATO is inviting influencers to come to their You know, think tank summit and memeify their conversations. That to me is playing a song for which the guitar chords are not already in people's heads. Whereas if you go on a popular sports podcast where they take a 10 minute break to talk about politics, and you insert yourself into that conversation, you are plucking on guitar chords that already exist in people's heads. It's a humility that I think elected officials need to have, which is, frankly, most people, most of the time, are not thinking about us and don't much care about us. And so we had better show up into their pattern of life and make ourselves relevant how they are consuming media, because they are not going to go out of their way to find what Jake Ockencloss is doing or saying in Washington DC.",
