    "turn_text": "Absolutely. That's a great question, Marshall. I would want to talk about preparations for combat in the Indo-Pacific, how we need to be building autonomous maritime and aerial systems and how we're decentralizing our military posture so that we can have smaller, more I think that's of interest to Americans and it helps make poignant this axis of autocracy we're facing between China, Russia, North Korea and Iran. Similarly, talk about Ukraine and how integrating Long-range strikes and drones can help Ukraine's infantry repel the Russian advance But I would also want to talk about some very close to home domestic issues housing first and foremost I think Marshall and I'm inclined to think you would agree that The best kernel of policy organizing in comms that Democrats have to build on is the YIMBY movement at the local level. A pro-builder, abundant, agenda policy that talks about lowering costs for an important share of wallet by building things again. And so I'd want to talk about how we can take the YINB movement and spiral that into a broader policy agenda for Democrats in energy, in tech folk education, in health care, and in other sectors that really matter to people. And then finally, and you're going to You might be surprised about this. I'd want to talk about fiscal responsibility in balancing the budget. This is something that Republicans have just vacated the playing field on this issue, right? And you've got these proposals from Musk and Rama Swami talking about doge, but they're coming across as unserious because they're talking about cutting $2 trillion annually, which is wiping out all discretionary funding. So like no air traffic controllers, no head start slots. It's not a serious proposal. They have got no appropriations authority. They have no executive branch authority. If they want to cut down on a waste fraud abuse, go ahead. I'm rooting for them, but they're not coming across as really competent right now. But it's a serious issue. We're adding a trillion dollars of debt every year. And I think young people get that it's not sustainable and that Social Security and Medicare are not going to be there for them. And I think they're actually ready to digest pretty nuanced conversations about how do we make Medicare viable 50 years from now? How do we make Social Security work?",
