    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "It really does, and it's a huge opportunity for us to come across one, I think to take two different lanes from Republicans. One is the reform angle. It used to be the government that Republicans were considered the sort of small government reform minded efficiency angle on government, that's actually not Donald Trump's approach. And again, I understand what Elon Musk is tweeting out right now, but he doesn't actually have any power. Donald Trump is sort of a big government guy, right? Like he wants to radically expand the federal deficit with tax policy and by expanding the entitlements program. So he is not a government reformer and obviously his abuses of power underscore how little he cares about good government. And then on the other side is the threat of freedom, which Kamala Harris I do think actually did a very effective job of starting to take that word back from Republicans. There is a strong libertarian impulse amongst the American electorate. People tend to be As the level of government gets higher, their willingness to be taxed for services gets lower, right? People are more willing at the local, slightly less at the state",
