    "turn_text": "less federal level. Both of these threads we should be pulling on as Democrats, and then just going forward to Americans and talking about how can we meaningfully uh, you know, keep government competent and yet also keep it from overreaching and collapsing in on itself. And that starts with a basic arithmetic of what our government does, because it really, at the federal level, the old joke is it's an insurance company with an army, and that's not far from the truth. Between Medicare and Social Security, that's about half the budget, and you got the military, it's about another almost a third or so, and you got net interest on the debt, which is really a secondary a consumer of resources. And then finally, you've got discretionary funding, which is about 15 to 20%. All of the fighting that we do in Washington is mostly about that 15 to 20%. And so anybody who claims that they're going to balance the budget by cutting head start slots or pulling back on affordable housing grants, they're just not being serious because it's not even meaningfully affecting the budget. It's all about Medicare and Social Security. And we can have that in-depth conversation, I think, with millennials and Gen Z.",
