  {
    "episode_id": 51087,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "You know, a couple of follow-ups on the budget. So I think the steelman defense of the lack of Democratic Party focus on the debt and the deficit of the past four years is basically like, look, like you and I, millennials, we can talk about this, right? Look at what happened during the 2008 financial crisis. We had a situation where people lost their homes, the economy was racked like the millennial story for like the millennial's a little older than you and I was really like, we had a terrible economy for three or four years. And while that was happening, you had the bowl of Simpson Commission, there's all this talk and hand-wringing about debt and spending, and it led to like a horrible economic situation for a couple of years. So the Biden folks, once again, to put on the Biden press secretary hat here, It's basically like, look, we actually didn't prioritize the debt and deficit because we prioritized actually making sure we could get back to full employment. So now our generation is going to have two competing narratives of thinking about how you respond to crises. The first one's going to be 2008 financial crisis one where 2021 to 2022 is a response to that. But now we're going to be also responding to 2022 to 2024 with inflation. So how do you think about the awkward dynamics here?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Democratic Party did not prioritize the national debt and deficit over the past four years.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The Biden administration prioritized achieving full employment over reducing the debt and deficit.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "During the 2008 financial crisis, millions lost their homes and the economy suffered severely for several years.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "In the aftermath of the 2008 crisis, there was significant public discussion and concern about government debt and spending.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Focusing on debt reduction after the 2008 crisis worsened the economic downturn.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "There will be two competing narratives shaping responses to economic crises: the 2008 model and the 2022–2024 inflation-driven model.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Economic recovery should take precedence over fiscal austerity during severe economic downturns.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The 2008 experience demonstrates that premature focus on debt reduction deepens recessions.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Full employment is a more urgent policy goal than balancing budgets during times of crisis.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Millennials share a collective historical memory shaped by the 2008 recession and its aftermath.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Public discourse around debt during crises often distracts from immediate economic stabilization needs.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
