    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 51087,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "It's supply side versus demand side. In the Obama era response was about boosting aggregate demand. Because what you had after the Great Recession, and this is a classic result of financial crises everywhere for all time, is when there's a banking crisis You deflate confidence on the demand side of the economy, people's willingness to purchase things, and you get a downward spiral where aggregate demand is going down and there by employment is going down. That is a real problem. That's what led to the Great Depression. And I think there's a lot of Monday morning quarterbacking that is accurately saying we should have spent more money in 2010, excuse me, to kickstart aggregate demands that you didn't have this long hangover in the 2010s. But the COVID era problem was supply side. We shut down the economy during the pandemic. We literally shut down the suppliers of goods and services. And then people went home and they started buying a bunch of goods on their laptops. And supply chains got snarled. Then we reopened the economy. Everybody shifted to trying to buy services because they'd been at home for a year. And there wasn't enough workforce to provide those services. And that led to inflation. And so it was two different problems. And what's interesting to me about the second problem, the supply side problem, is that to me it is a much better indicator of how we need to operate in the 2020s, which is to say we need to be supply side progressives. looking at the supply side of the economy to lower costs for people. So housing to me, first and foremost, we should build 10 million units of housing in this country. 10 million units of housing. That's largely about zoning reform, but also about offsite construction. But we should also be trying to build 1,000 nuclear power plants. We should be trying to build, like I said, many new tech vogue high schools. We should build more ships than the entire Chinese Navy has. They've outbuilt us over the last 10 years. It's an embarrassment. There's nothing that says American shipbuilding can't dominate the blue ocean again, but we should build it. And a president should declare that that's the national mission, and we should animate Americans that feel like they're building together for purpose.",
