  {
    "episode_id": 51087,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and I think the thing that's really interesting here, and this is we can pivot to your Twitter thread that you put out after everyone was giving their election takes. You actually kind of like, it's not that we converge in an idea at the same time, except you tweeted it so you get full credit, which is a loved your sort of delineation of like Coke versus diet Coke when it came to populism. So I'll let you explain what you never thought in a second, but this is something I talk a lot about. which is so like my family in law, they're in South Carolina, like they're super mega, they're people who like love RFK Jr. And it's been really helpful to sort of like talk and just like see and like experience of the way that they're thinking of things because the reason why I bring in this sort of diet versus Coca-Cola, like my interpretation of it is I think there's this effort to say, look, it's been clear that there's this broad population of populist Americans who are looking to upend the system, do all these new things. The Biden approach didn't work. The center left in approach didn't work. So what we need is left-wing populism to speak to those voters. And I think what people would say that intellectually, that tracks, that's a defensible op-ed. I think what you actually experience when you talk to actual people in this white quadrant is, guess what? The type of populism they want is RFK Jr. going into the FDA and basically burning the place down. That is just something that you just fundamentally cannot do if you're a center left to left government because ultimately you need government to work. That's the whole thing. That's why you're a Democrat. I'd love to get your take on this whole broader phenomenon.",
