  {
    "episode_id": 51087,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "I mean, frankly, you kind of just put it better than I could. I mean, I totally agree with you, which is there is obviously the Democrats are having a debate right now about did wokeness kind of cost Democrats the election. And we can have that conversation about the Democratic version of identity politics and the Republican version of identity politics and how I think really you should be rejecting both, frankly. What concerns me, actually, though, is not that debate because I think debate is healthy, and we should be talking about it. What concerns me is an increasing consensus that I'm detecting from Democrats, which is, oh, you know, the problem is we're not populist enough. We've got to be the populist party. And like you said, Marshall, my view is that's Diet Coke. People ordered a Coca-Cola, and they have a party for that. I think electoral strategy starts with knowing who your voters are not going to be. If you are a voter who wants RFK to go in and tear up the FDA, if you are a voter who thinks that an individual should buy a assault weapon at Walmart with no questions asked, if you're a voter who wants dreamers to be deported without due process, we're just not going to be your party. And that kind of clear delineation on values is critical for then us to be able to architect our own message about economic performance, because ultimately I think voters really respond to economic performance more than they do to economic policies, and a supply side progressive agenda will deliver that performance.",
