  {
    "episode_id": 51087,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "I do think that you can channel it, though, and the way I think about that is it goes back to Democrats reclaiming freedom and being the reformers, which is to say, we can talk about an economy that is for builders and doers, not an economy of nimbies and middlemen. We can talk about an economy that is not captured by either corporate or public sector special interests, where anybody can build something. And I talk about that as an economy that works like Legos, not Monopoly. And then we can go sector by sector and talk about the examples. So for example, in housing, the NIMBYs, those who can veto missing middle housing by organizing 15 people to come to a land use meeting, those are the NIMBYs. And we've got to be able to build in this country the housing that we need. If you look at health care, the middlemen are the pharmacy benefit managers who literally got an exemption in the 1990s from a law called the anti-kickback statute. They asked to not be included in that law. And when an industry comes to Congress and says, hey, you guys have this law about not taking kickbacks, we would super like a waiver from that. The reasonable response is not, yeah, sure, let's strap that up for you. The reasonable response is, no, stop taking kickbacks. Well, not surprisingly for the last 25 years, but they've been taken kickbacks. They call it rebates. Let's fight that and channel, I think, righteous populist anger against that corporate middlemen behavior and instead celebrate the builders of the healthcare sector, those who invent new drugs, those who invent new medical devices, those physicians and nurses who are providing life-saving treatment. Same thing, you look in the big tech sector, right? In the 1990s, we gave Section 230 immunity to the social media corporations or what would become the social media corporations. Now maybe in the 1990s that was a good idea, but what it has since become is the most wealthy powerful corporations in the history of the world have zero duty of care to their young users on their platform. Deep fake pornography, cyber harassment, bullying, intimate privacy violations, they don't care. And we've got to attack that special interest exemption that they have. So we can channel some of that, I think, anger about an economy that does not feel like it's a level playing field, but still have a pro-builder agenda where we're supporting people doing real things, whether it's new companies in the software space or AI or blockchain derived, rather than just rewarding these corporate walled-off networks that Facebook and Google have created.",
