  {
    "episode_id": 51087,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "There's a lot to cover in that terrain. The area that I think is probably most fruitful to focus on is in healthcare. One, because Democrats start with a trust advantage on healthcare. You ask voters, which issues do you care about and then which issues do you trust M's or Republicans on, and general Democrats have a trust surplus on healthcare, which is an important place to start. Two, and more importantly, Warren Buffett famously called health care the tapeworm eating the economy. And unfortunately, the tapeworm's only gotten hungrier since he said that we are seeing a situation now where Americans spend $4.5 trillion every year on health care. That's the size of Germany's economy is within the US economy. $20, $25,000 a year going towards health insurance premiums. And then when you do get sick, your out-of-pocket costs add significantly to those expenditures. Depending on how you calculate it, People are spending as much as 30% of their pay on health insurance as a country right now. I'm talking to employers all across my district who are increasingly feeling suffocated about it. So we have to do something. And when you look at what is driving this healthcare inflation, as I talked about it, partly as the pharmacy benefit managers who are the middlemen of drug pricing, but actually the greatest share of inflation is coming from hospital prices. And hospital prices have gone up so much, partly because we've allowed so much consolidation in that space. And we are going to need more state level, anti-consolidation, both litigation and legislative energy, to prevent this huge agglomeration of providers into one mega entity that can basically dictate prices.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
