    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "I really think it's, the debate in the 2010s was about coverage, getting more people covered. That remains a debate, we gotta get Medicaid expanded in the states where it has not yet done. But to me, the primary axis of debate with healthcare now is no longer coverage, it is cost. Because if people are paying $25,000, $30,000 a year for their health insurance, at a certain point, it stops feeling like you're covered at that point, right? The cost becomes suffocating. And I think Democrats in the same way that I think we really earned the right to be the party of health care by expanding coverage. We now need to earn that right in the next decade by being the party that lowers health care costs. And that's going to require, I think, some pretty significant changes at the state level as well as the federal level and how we regulate health care consolidation. I mean, you look at Medicare for an advantage, for example. And here, the Biden administration did some good work in their coding intensity adjustments where they basically were cracking down in their payments. But what you see is United Health Group and Signal Express scripts and these other private health plans have vertically integrated to the extent where they're all self-dealing and they are inflating how risky they claim their populations are, and then they're footing taxpayers with the bill. And this is contrary to the spirit of Medicare Advantage. The spirit of Medicare Advantage was that you're going to get the best of the public and private sectors. You're going to get the efficiency and the innovation of the private sector with the guaranteed stability of the public sector that if you're sick, you're going to be able to get health care that you need. And the private sector has been taking advantage of that deal. And I do think there's more work to be done to crack down on these private health plans.",
