    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and I just want to say I'm glad I'm speaking to an elected official because this is sort of a stick in a rant I've had going on the podcast for a while, but I haven't actually given it to an elected official. So basically, I've done a lot of like abundance agenda pushing on this podcast, lots of like supply side liberalism pushing on this podcast and people who just email me saying like, hey, you don't normally take like particularly aggressive stands on things. Like, why are you like really like dialing in on this? And my answer is, you know, I was back in high school. I was like a, you know, student government want to be kids. So I was youth governor of Oregon. I went to Boyce Nation. I very much like did that trick. I kind of wanted to be in your position when I grew up. So I just really think I still have kept that like millennial politician side of my brain activated. And I think by the abundance agenda matter so much is it kind of refocuses that brain away from where we've been directed the past 30, 40 years. It's like you just said costs, things cost too much. Your job, think about like, let's say we're talking about the 2000s, right? Like if you just quizzed our like student government peers, what they would say is like, our job is to go to Congress or go to the governor's office and pass this big bill that gives this right or gets this big program, fulfills this promise of the new deal or the great society, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera. And I don't think what developed alongside that is just this understanding of like, hey, like, costs matter. Costs aren't just like a Republican sort of like Reagan and I neoliberal thing. This is something you should be thinking about. And when I talk to people about these abundance episodes, I just get so much input from millennial politicians who straight up say, I did not have that framework in the back of my mind. So I just really want to give you the chance just to take over the mic for me and just run with this cost thing. Because it's so, so, so, so important and it can't be understated.",
