    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "policy response is to expand supply. Because what you are going to do is you are going to drive down costs without creating any of the shortages or inflation that's surging demand would. And this is a critical lesson, because Democrats too often fall back into what I call the 1945, or rather the 1939 mindset, which is because of the incredible outsized statute that FDR and the New Deal has in the party, Democrats too often look at every single ailment in our policy realm and say, the answer is stimulating demand. We have child care is too expensive. What we should go out there and do is hand every family a check for child care. Houses are too expensive, what we should go out do is hand every new home buyer a check for buying a new home. Well, if you keep supply fixed and you give everybody extra money to buy a fixed supply of things, what happens? Just the price goes up. That's not a political statement. That's just a statement about how supply and demand graphs function. And so what we have to be doing is things like what Massachusetts did with childcare, which is they took that block grant of money. And instead of giving every family a check, they did direct to provider grants where they helped cover the overhead expenses of existing childcare providers so that they could hire more teachers, create more childcare slots, have more retention and flexibility in their employment options. They didn't have to fire people every school year. And it's worked incredibly well. more work to do, obviously, but those kinds of policy interventions need to be the default presumption for Democrats. And at the end of the day, what it's really about is Beaumel's Cost Disease, which you may or may not be familiar with, but I think should be mandatory reading for every policymaker in economics, because if you're not treating Beaumel's Cost Disease in any given sector, you're going to continue to see inflation.",
