    "turn_text": "What almost cost disease is the observation that sectors that are very services intensive steadily consume a greater share of wallet over time because there is cross elasticity of demand for employees across sectors. And some sectors like, for example, TD manufacturing see incredible gains in productivity because they can automate the process and they go down the production cost curve. And every given unit of that Good becomes cheaper to produce and in sectors that have higher productivity those employees can command higher wages and that wage premium pulls on every other sector because people can always switch jobs and so over time you've got people in low productivity sectors who are commanding wages that reflect if they were in high productivity sectors. There's some more caveats here about consumer intent, but that's the high level answer. And I want to be clear here that the word productivity does not have a moral sheen to it. There are very low, quote unquote, low productivity sectors like childcare, which are incredibly virtuous. And there are high productivity sectors like pill-pressing fentanyl, which is very high productivity that are horrible things to do, right? So it's not a good or bad. It's just literally the amount of output per hour of input. And the challenge we have is that healthcare, education, government are three sectors that have experienced very low productivity growth over the last 70 years and are consuming ever greater share of wallet because of that.",
