    "turn_text": "Yeah. And I just got to say, and this is the last big word for me on the cost area thing, I've just become so obsessed with the cost discussion because frankly, it's the one to borrow a term from the left, like the most reflects like the lived experience of me and my peers. So like, I'm going to have a kid soon, like very pumped for that, but you know, I moved from New York to Austin, Texas, and I moved from downtown into the suburbs. And the whole reason why I moved from New York City to Austin, Texas, is that it's supposed to solve all these problems. I'm not doing tiger mom style competitions over private school slots. There's no subway issues. I could drive her where it's really great. And we move into this new suburb. And immediately, there's a two and a half year wait for child care. for preschool for always like different things. So even if you just gave me, which I don't deserve because my income's too high, but like, well, sorry, I'm in favor of universal programs. So like, that's fine. I get like a check, I get some fair amount of a check for childcare. It doesn't actually impact the issue because there just aren't enough. Even in like upper middle class like suburbs where you think this would be fine, there literally are just like not enough day care spaces for everyone. And that's that story. The other story, and this is when I, I don't identify as EMB for like complicated cultural reasons. I think it's a little too like San Francisco coded. So, but like that said, if you're, but my defense of the MBs are if they give me a single specific policy proposal, I usually am like, okay, I'm fine with that. So that's my like, addendum on MBism. But like, look, when I talk with my friends, like who just work like normal, like jobs in Austin, what they'll talk about is they're like, yeah, man, like it sucks. My like, girlfriend and I were about to get engaged and she super wants to like live in the city because we're like 29 or 30. We don't want to like go off to the suburbs and be home bodies. But we can't afford a house there and the house we can't afford is like 50 minutes drive outside of Austin and some like, you know, how a whole like excerpt. So it's not even just like the suburbs. You don't even further and further and further. So that's them talking about how there are not starter homes in Austin, Texas that they can afford, that they live in for four or five years. They have a kid, then they pivot to the suburbs that go from there. But like, once again, these are reflecting the cost and like abundant story that I'm so obsessed with.",
