    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "And it goes back to, I hear you on the coding of UMB, I do proud to declare myself a UMB. But it goes back to, I think, the reason why the arguments put forward by that movement, I think, can be fractal, why they can spiral out to other issues and at higher levels of government. Because what they said is they identified a problem that was acute for people, the cost of housing. They looked at existing solutions, which was basically subsidizing an existing stock of housing through affordable housing tax credits and other subsidies. And they said, that's not going to work. And here is the answer that will work, which is a supply side intervention to eliminate zoning or to relax zoning restrictions, excuse me, and then to build more housing. And it works. It works. Look at a city like Tokyo, for example, where the The rent as a function of wages makes it actually quite affordable to live there because they built a lot. I mean, the modern Manhattan, which people think of as a product unto itself that's highly desirable, would never have been able to be built under current zoning laws. There's also another element here, and now we're going down a zoning rabbit hole. But I think it's important to reflect that the zoning that was put in place post World War II has to a large extent undermined our civic cohesion because it's created these monocultures as opposed to the mixed use granular walkable downtowns that used to exist before World War II, where people from all walks of life were bumping into each other, and where there was a sense of community that was, I think, much more robust than exists in most average suburbs. And so, lightening our zoning restrictions not just lowers the cost of housing, I also think it literally creates stronger towns.",
