    "turn_text": "here's my, here's my pushback on that though is, listen, I got three little kids and I drive all over the place. My problem with zoning codes, as they are currently written, is that they preclude pluralism because they insist on one right way of living. Whereas if you had land use policy that said, yeah, within reasonable guardrails about affecting other people's enjoyment of living, build what you want. Yeah. If Steve wants to build a single family house, he can build a single family house. But what you can't do is insist that everybody else has to have a single family house. It's really, to me, a matter of freedom. And sitting there with things like parking minimums, right? Doesn't mean you can't build parking if you get rid of parking minimums. It just means that you can't insist that everybody else internalize the cost of driving a car.",
