    "turn_text": "a little bit older, they have a Facebook group, they all see each other walking their dogs, boom, they're organized, and they are deeply committed because they feel that this, they perceive that this impacts their quality of life significantly. So they are motivated and they are organized almost by default. YIMB's by contrast are speaking for a diffuse yet to be realized kind of person out there who maybe wants to buy some house that hasn't been built yet right and they're speaking kind of on behalf of the of the public good of lower housing costs if you increase supply enough that's a very challenging thing to do and yet they have done it they have done it right they have successfully changed zoning laws i think by organizing by bringing to bear evidence and by being persistent and effective at how they lobby at the state and local level. So I think there's absolutely lessons to be drawn there. When it comes to the groups, though, I think we have to complicate the narrative by adding in two other dimensions of modern politics, which is one gerrymandering. Out of the 435 congressional seats, only about 35 or competitive on election day, right, on general election day. So that means 400 members of Congress are concerned about their primary, not their general election. So if you combine gerrymandering with the existence of partisan primaries and then with the groups, what you start to see is for any given member of Congress or any given legislator at the state level, most likely what they're actually oriented to is their primary election, which is low turnout, highly ideological voters, where those groups probably do actually have resonance. And that's part of what gives the groups, I think, outsize influence, is that most elected officials are oriented towards a very ideologically charged, low turnout electorate for their primary. So we actually have to, I think, solve this problem, not just by pushing back harder on maybe groups that have phantom constituencies, but also by ending the atrocious gerrymandering that occurs in places like North Carolina. And second, getting rid of the partisan primary system in favor of what Alaska did with Final Five Building.",
