    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "So in this last few minutes we have together, I want to do a couple topics of no particular order. So I hope I'm not oversharing and filing. I'm definitely breaking Chatham House rules. But you and I were at an event back in the spring. And you said something that really, really won me over, which is you really talked about the need for like a reform movement in this country that really like apes and speaks to the energy with the progressive movement of like the 1890s and 1910s, those different areas. A real need for political reform in our system, you just hinted at that with your point on gerrymandering and the way we run our elections. But even if in Congress, right, like there's a lot of energy and opportunity here. I think what's difficult for me is And this is just because I talked to regular people, I ran into a podcast listener or a children's birthday party. So I actually don't just talk to people that I talk to actual people is I understand what people say when they talk about term limits and congressional stock trading bans, but the side of me that just actually lived in DC and works in think tanks and actually knows how this works, knows what quite to be super frank with you. Term limits don't make anything different. We've tried them in California. We've run the experiment. They're a bunch of very easy, poly-sigh, one-on-one reasons why this isn't going to do. This woman has changed her way, describing actually if anything could make things worse. And then the Congressional Stock Trading ban of, look, I'm not going to defend the policy. It's a bad look, but I promise you, we didn't pass the CHIPS Act so that Nancy Pelosi could make a bunch of money. I promise you debate Ukraine all you want. We aren't backing Ukraine so as someone can make one half of defense stocks there. I think the public has a gap between their fair critiques of the status quo and the impacts you would actually do. So here's to hear from you. What's the way that we can And maybe you actually defend the stock trading ban and term limits. I'd love to hear that from you. But what is the way we could square the public's demand for reform with, I think, reforms that actually matter and make a difference? Because going this way, does anyone know? This is to the Californian listeners. You guys have had term limits for the past 20 years. Do you feel any better about your democracy? Do you think you feel represent? I don't think that's true at all. So how do you think we could square that dynamic?",
