    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51087.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 49,
    "turn_text": "I think about it in three different veins, at least three different veins. And briefly, those would be capture, competition, and abundance. So trying to reform government one to prevent regulatory or special interest capture, which I think the stock trading ban falls under that, but more broadly, why can't we ban PFAS production? We've got companies out there that are producing stainless-resistant carpets that have known carcinogens in them. Why isn't that banned? I think that's a totally legitimate critique of Washington DC right now. That should be illegal. And to the extent that that is because of regulatory capture or because there's too much vested power in Washington from these major chemical manufacturers, absolutely we should take that on as reformers. Number two is abundance. So we talked about this, whether it's energy, whether it's health care, whether it's housing, whether it's education. What is very clear is that oftentimes the red tape and the regulations are in the habit of the supply side of the equation. And supply side progressivism should be actively rooting out the vitocracy, the nodes where small numbers of individuals can prevent something from happening. I live in Massachusetts. It took us 15 years, and we still haven't built a transmission line from Quebec, which has an unbelievable amount of hydropower to Massachusetts, which is basically an energy island, because of a few people in Maine who litigated the thing half to death, and by the way, that was funded by special interests. absolutely attacking that those nodes of autocracy that prevent abundance. And then finally, competition. I think in general, we too often try to impose a one-size-fits-all approach on a nation of 330 million people with 50 states where people's moorays and preferences are wildly different. We should allow for more competition and service providers, and that requires reform as well.",
