    "turn_text": "So for the last question, I'd love to just get your perspective on the quote unquote, wokeness debate. I'm in this weird position where, like, despite all of my conservative podcast and work affiliations, I'm kind of stuck in the realignment where, like, My sort of like, hot take is that if you're like a pro Ukraine, pro Taiwan, those different areas, arsenal of democracy, what's build things, select conductors, you're kind of in the center left, given where things are moving. And I think my affiliations will probably have to adjust over time, but that's sort of my internal cope. I'm still like someone who finds myself like on like heterodox center left here. So I know of an on paper, I'm supposed to say that like, We need to have this big fight over wokeness with Democrats and sister soldier moments galore, but I also like really like the political part of me thinks that's actually not helpful. And I think that like we have the idea of the sister soldier moment, but then there's like, did it actually matter? Because like what I think actually matter, right? No one actually saw the sister soldier moment happen. John Gans had a great book about this recently when the clock broke. And what really mattered is that Bill Clinton at a time of the Democratic Party was seen as too far left was just a normal guy who Americans could trust to like fairly adjudicate cry cultural issues. And I think if I was like a Democratic congressman, I wouldn't be saying to myself, how do I pick a fight with like, you know, Latino groups that say Latin next? Or how do I pick a fight over like trans athletes? And I would just think we say like, Hey guys, I'm normal and I'm chill. How do you just, here's how you just like think about this at a meta level.",
