    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "No, but once again, Marshall, you're kind of saying it better than I could. I view it the same way, really, which is one, Democrats shouldn't take the bait on the Republicans framing of these issues, right? As we said at the beginning of this conversation, there's a section of the electorate that Democrats are not going to compete for because they're not going to share our values, and that's part of party politics. We do think, as a party, that, you know, People should be able to live in the body that they identify with that is part of pushing the envelope of freedom farther That was first promised by the founders that the circumstances of your burst are not determined the condition of your life We're not gonna insult immigrants. We're not gonna laugh at misogynistic jokes, right? Like so To a certain extent, we shouldn't take the bait, we shouldn't try to like dial it down to the point where it's indistinguishable or where we're kind of apologizing for it. We are going to be proudly pushing forward. At the same time, just as I think the Christian nationalism of MAGA is ultimately not going to appeal to the majority of Americans, I don't think identity politics on the left appeals to the majority of Americans. In fact, I know that it doesn't, and at times I have wedged there. But I don't think we make this the frame of our argument to the American people. I'll give you an example of this in a specific issue. So when it comes to education right now, an issue that is dominating politically is the issue of participation of trans women in women's sports. You know, we got to figure that out, right? Like, sport by sport, age bracket by age bracket, we need to put in science-driven rules that ensure fair play and fair participation. But you know what we're not talking about? We're not talking about the fact that we closed the schools for two years. And in Massachusetts, we've lost 20 years worth of math and literacy gains. And it's going to take us 10 years to get those gains back. I'm going to go hazard a guess that the parents in my district care a lot more about math and literacy and science than they do about this other issue. And yet we're not giving them a plan for how are we going to scale up one to one mastery tutoring? How are we going to extend school hours in the summertime or after school? How are we going to make sure that your kid is succeeding? And instead, I think we run the risk of looking like we're getting distracted yet again by an issue that is, I think, more esoteric.",
